{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The problem was in `bucket_of` in `repo/histogram.py`. The docstring says values on the upper bound (`value == width * n_buckets`) should map to the last bucket, but the implementation only did `value // width` and never used `n_buckets`. For `bucket_of(30, 10, 3)` that returned `3`, which is out of range for a 3-bucket histogram and made `histogram_summary` in `repo/rows.py` raise `IndexError` when it incremented `counts[bucket_of(...)]`.\n\n**Change:** after computing `idx = value // width`, if `idx >= n_buckets`, return `n_buckets - 1`. Interior values are unchanged (e.g. `5` with width `10` still maps to bucket `0`). `old_bucket` delegates to `bucket_of`, so it picks up the same behavior.\n\nAll visible tests pass, and a quick check of `histogram_summary([30, 29, 10], 10, 3)` yields `counts: [0, 1, 2]` without crashing."}]},"session_id":"e1ce2eff-9fac-479d-bb52-96ff720fb78a"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":43489,"duration_api_ms":43489,"is_error":false,"result":"Inspecting the histogram implementation and related helpers.\n\n\nFixing `bucket_of`: it ignores `n_buckets` and returns `3` for the top edge (`30 // 10`), causing index errors in `histogram_summary`. Clamping the index to `n_buckets - 1`.\n\n\nThe problem was in `bucket_of` in `repo/histogram.py`. The docstring says values on the upper bound (`value == width * n_buckets`) should map to the last bucket, but the implementation only did `value // width` and never used `n_buckets`. For `bucket_of(30, 10, 3)` that returned `3`, which is out of range for a 3-bucket histogram and made `histogram_summary` in `repo/rows.py` raise `IndexError` when it incremented `counts[bucket_of(...)]`.\n\n**Change:** after computing `idx = value // width`, if `idx >= n_buckets`, return `n_buckets - 1`. Interior values are unchanged (e.g. `5` with width `10` still maps to bucket `0`). `old_bucket` delegates to `bucket_of`, so it picks up the same behavior.\n\nAll visible tests pass, and a quick check of `histogram_summary([30, 29, 10], 10, 3)` yields `counts: [0, 1, 2]` without crashing.","session_id":"e1ce2eff-9fac-479d-bb52-96ff720fb78a","request_id":"16bd3f13-97fb-4d9b-9591-6359952650f1","usage":{"inputTokens":12539,"outputTokens":1530,"cacheReadTokens":78533,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
